<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of the Cross</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a>–<a href="/years/1591">1591</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of the Cross was a Spanish Carmelite friar, priest and mystic who worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teresa-of-avila">Teresa of Ávila</a> to reform the Carmelite order. Imprisoned by opponents of the reform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in 1577–1578, he composed there part of his Spiritual Canticle. His poems and commentaries, including The Dark Night of the Soul, are among the summits of Spanish literature and Christian mysticism. He was canonised in 1726.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-of-the-cross.jpg" alt="John of the Cross" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zurbar%C3%A1n_(atribuido)-John_of_the_Cross-1656.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
